"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":202,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The fix works. In all four re-runs, every saved row now carries its own frame's buffer number in column 0. X/Y/Z stayed bit-identical, and nothing was lost or damaged beyond what overload already skipped. Report: `tools/bench/lean_bench/lean_report_p6fix.md`.\n\n**What was wrong.** Loop 1.2 read four per-slot values from the ring before the frame it was about to track had arrived. So when it kept up, the row got the previous occupant's value (frame k-20). The four values are `BufDiff`, `TransPos`, `RotPos` and `FrameIdx`. Column 0 actually comes from `BufDiff`, not `FrameIdx` as the P6-3 report said. All four were read the same wrong way, so all four are fixed.\n\n**The fix.** I added one new frame at the front of the existing flat sequence that holds the second sequence-number check (n2). I moved those four reads and their index lookups into it. They now run after the frame is picked (n1) and before n2, inside the same check that already protects the image. Nothing was copied in, and no calculation node was touched. The edge set (every wire end-to-end) matches the original bed exactly, read live and again from the saved file in a fresh LabVIEW. Fixed VI: `claudeDev\\bench\\lean\\D1_ring_p6fix_20261013_231910.vi`, md5 3e847397eb120860adb2e868792345f7.\n\n**Results:**\n\n| condition | rows with their own number | before the fix | lost | damaged |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| P6-2 re-run (normal load) | 2537/2537 | 0/2538 | 0 | 0 |\n| gapped source (numbers 2k+1) | 2537/2537 | 0/2537 | 0 | 0 |\n| W40 (normal load + 40 ms) | 2540/2540 | 0/2540 | 0 | 0 |\n| W100 (overload) | 1432/1432 | 1430/1431 | 1097 | 0 |\n\n- **X/Y/Z:** 10046/10046 rows bit-identical.\n- **TransPos/RotPos:** identical on every row after device init. Row 0 trans now holds a real stage reading (about 21.6–21.7 mm against S1's 21.6852) instead of 0.0.\n- **Overload:** pick and discard behaviour is unchanged as you asked. The 1097 lost frames are 356 single skips and 39 jumps of 19, the same pattern as P6-3. Latest-wins (U8) is still open.\n\n**Things you should know:**\n- **Builds:** the build took five runs; runs 1–4 stopped before saving. The causes were a For-loop input tunnel that LabVIEW deleted during the move (the value now goes in through a new tunnel), and 8 leftover Invoke nodes the move tool leaves behind (now deleted). Their unsaved copies were checked by md5 and removed.\n- **Cal file check:** the P6-2 compare flags the cal file as different. The only reason is that the date text is one byte longer (`오후 11:55:22` vs `오후 2:33:18`), which shifts every later byte. Compared after the date field, the bytes are identical.\n- **Motor sessions:** I never ran `motor_gate --session start|end` myself, and I checked that `motor_session.json` existed before each leg. However, the unchanged replay leg program (`diag_c104_leg.py` → v5) runs its own `--session start` before and after each run. That re-applies the same limits and repeats the PI reference move. It never ends the session, so the limits stayed on throughout. Every readback was OK (PI limits 0..39). Releasing the limits is left to the queue.\n- **Other:** input md5s are equal at start and end (24/24). LabVIEW is closed (0 processes). Nothing was committed. One leftover draft file, `p6fix_report_s4.md`, can be deleted; removing it needed an approval this run didn't have.\n\nRESULT {\"p6fix\": \"PASS\", \"col0_own\": {\"p62\": \"2537/2537\", \"gap\": \"2537/2537\", \"w40\": \"2540/2540\", \"w100\": \"1432/1432\"}, \"xyz\": \"10046/10046\", \"minutes\": 148}","ttft_ms":1610,"type":"result","duration_ms":8310413,"uuid":"8cd51b3f-d915-418f-acfd-f94073d8fde5","ttft_stream_ms":1019,"time_to_request_ms":71,"first_content_frame_ms":1019,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
